**What is claimed is:**

1. A method for measuring packet transmission latency in a wireless communication system, the method comprising:  
   obtaining, by a first communication device, a first timestamp associated with transmission of an uplink packet from the first communication device to a second communication device;  
   obtaining a second timestamp associated with reception of the uplink packet at the second communication device;  
   obtaining, by the second communication device, a third timestamp associated with transmission of a downlink packet from the second communication device to the first communication device;  
   obtaining a fourth timestamp associated with reception of the downlink packet at the first communication device;  
   determining an uplink latency based at least in part on the first timestamp and the second timestamp; and  
   determining a downlink latency based at least in part on the third timestamp and the fourth timestamp.

2. The method of claim 1, further comprising synchronizing respective clocks of the first communication device and the second communication device or determining a clock-offset value between the respective clocks.

3. The method of claim 2, wherein determining the uplink latency and the downlink latency comprises compensating at least one of the timestamps based on the clock-offset value.

4. The method of claim 1, wherein at least one of the first, second, third, or fourth timestamps is generated at a physical layer, a medium-access-control layer, a radio-link-control layer, a packet-data-convergence-protocol layer, or an application layer.

5. The method of claim 1, wherein the first timestamp is included in the uplink packet or in control signaling associated with the uplink packet, and wherein the third timestamp is included in the downlink packet or in control signaling associated with the downlink packet.

6. The method of claim 1, further comprising transmitting, between the first communication device and the second communication device, a latency report comprising at least one of the timestamps, the uplink latency, the downlink latency, a packet identifier, a sequence number, or a clock-offset value.

7. The method of claim 1, wherein the downlink packet comprises an acknowledgment responsive to the uplink packet, and the method further comprises determining a round-trip latency based on the first timestamp and the fourth timestamp.

8. The method of claim 7, further comprising excluding, from the round-trip latency, a processing interval between reception of the uplink packet and transmission of the downlink packet.

9. The method of claim 1, wherein obtaining a timestamp comprises capturing the timestamp in response to at least one of:  
   delivery of a packet to a protocol layer;  
   scheduling of the packet for transmission;  
   transfer of the packet to radio-frequency circuitry;  
   transmission of a first symbol carrying the packet;  
   reception of a first symbol carrying the packet; or  
   successful decoding of the packet.

10. The method of claim 1, further comprising associating the first and second timestamps with a common uplink packet identifier and associating the third and fourth timestamps with a common downlink packet identifier.

11. The method of claim 1, further comprising determining, for a plurality of uplink packets and a plurality of downlink packets, at least one latency statistic comprising an average latency, a minimum latency, a maximum latency, a latency variance, a latency percentile, or a jitter value.

12. The method of claim 1, further comprising adjusting, based on the uplink latency or the downlink latency, at least one of a scheduling parameter, a retransmission parameter, a modulation and coding scheme, a routing selection, a quality-of-service parameter, or a packet prioritization parameter.

13. A communication apparatus comprising:  
   communication circuitry configured to transmit and receive uplink and downlink packets;  
   timestamping circuitry configured to generate timestamps corresponding to transmission or reception events for the uplink and downlink packets; and  
   one or more processors configured to:  
   correlate a transmission timestamp and a reception timestamp for an uplink packet;  
   correlate a transmission timestamp and a reception timestamp for a downlink packet;  
   determine an uplink transmission latency based on the correlated timestamps for the uplink packet; and  
   determine a downlink transmission latency based on the correlated timestamps for the downlink packet.

14. The communication apparatus of claim 13, wherein the one or more processors are further configured to compensate the correlated timestamps for clock offset, clock drift, propagation delay, retransmission delay, buffering delay, scheduling delay, or processing delay.

15. The communication apparatus of claim 13, wherein the communication apparatus comprises a user equipment, a base station, an access point, a relay, a network node, or a server.

16. The communication apparatus of claim 13, wherein the timestamping circuitry comprises a hardware timestamp unit coupled to a radio interface and configured to generate a timestamp at a defined packet-boundary or symbol-boundary event.

17. The communication apparatus of claim 13, wherein the one or more processors are configured to distinguish an over-the-air latency from an end-to-end latency based on timestamps captured at different protocol layers or processing locations.

18. A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of a communication device, cause the communication device to:  
   record a local transmission timestamp for a packet transmitted in a first link direction;  
   receive a remote reception timestamp for the packet;  
   receive a remote transmission timestamp for another packet transmitted in a second link direction opposite the first link direction;  
   record a local reception timestamp for the other packet; and  
   determine respective packet transmission latencies for the first link direction and the second link direction using the local and remote timestamps.

19. The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 18, wherein the first link direction is an uplink and the second link direction is a downlink, or wherein the first link direction is a downlink and the second link direction is an uplink.

20. The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 18, wherein the instructions further cause the communication device to validate a latency measurement based on timestamp accuracy information, clock-synchronization quality, packet correlation information, or a retransmission status.
